sort_template.h: the generic qsort swaps pointer-sized units, so pointers keep their tag (intcap plan, Phase D).

pg_qsort is sort_template.h with ST_ELEMENT_TYPE_VOID, and its swap moves an element one
char at a time. On Capstone a pointer copied as bytes comes back without its capability
tag. bulk_write.c sorts its pending pages with qsort before WAL-logging them, and
log_newpages then registered an untagged page: XLogRecordAssemble faulted (cause 24) on
lhu 0xc(page). A probe in XLogRegisterBlock caught the page (0xd9d21000, untagged) with
log_newpages as caller.

An element that holds a pointer is pointer-aligned, and its size is a multiple of the
pointer size. So when both element addresses and the size allow it, the swap moves
void * units, which the compiler copies as capabilities. Anything else keeps the byte
loop.

--- a/src/include/lib/sort_template.h
+++ b/src/include/lib/sort_template.h
@@ -281,6 +281,30 @@
 static inline void
 ST_SWAPN(ST_POINTER_TYPE * a, ST_POINTER_TYPE * b, size_t n)
 {
+#if defined(ST_ELEMENT_TYPE_VOID) && defined(__SIZEOF_INTCAP__)
+	/*
+	 * capstone: with a void element type this loop swaps bytes, and a pointer
+	 * moved a byte at a time loses its capability tag (bulk_write's qsort of
+	 * pending pages handed log_newpages an untagged page). An element that
+	 * holds a pointer is pointer-aligned and a multiple of its size, so swap
+	 * in pointer-sized units whenever both elements and the size allow it.
+	 */
+	if ((uintptr_t) a % sizeof(void *) == 0 && (uintptr_t) b % sizeof(void *) == 0 &&
+		n % sizeof(void *) == 0)
+	{
+		void	  **pa = (void **) a;
+		void	  **pb = (void **) b;
+
+		for (size_t i = 0; i < n / sizeof(void *); ++i)
+		{
+			void	   *tmp = pa[i];
+
+			pa[i] = pb[i];
+			pb[i] = tmp;
+		}
+		return;
+	}
+#endif
 	for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
 		ST_SWAP(&a[i], &b[i]);
 }
